= Strategy — Wave 1.5

Wave 1.5 adds systems that change *how you think* about Bannerlord. These are not convenience mods. They require you to adapt your strategy.

== Campaign Map Mastery

=== Let Your Companions Work

With *Party AI Controls* and *Useful Companions*, your companion-led parties become genuine strategic assets. Set patrol routes along your borders. Designate defensive zones around vulnerable villages. Order specific recruitment behaviors — one party recruits cavalry, another recruits archers. Set engagement rules so your scout party doesn't suicide into a 500-man army.

Your quartermaster auto-buys food. Your governor manages town prosperity to prevent starvation. Your caravan leaders actually improve at their jobs. Your companions earn XP from these tasks — the surgeon who has treated a thousand wounded is genuinely better at it than one who has sat in a keep.

The strategic payoff: You stop being the only competent person in Calradia. Your organization functions while you are fighting on the other side of the continent. This is the difference between a warband and a kingdom.

=== AI That Fights Smart

*Art of War* fixes the campaign AI's worst behaviors. Armies no longer wander aimlessly after capturing a settlement — they patrol the last-attacked location. When a village is being raided, nearby armies detach parties to defend it. Mercenary clans are not wasted on garrison duty. Siege targeting makes strategic sense.

*AI Values Life* and *Surrender Tweaks* create a complete capture ecology. Bandits and outmatched parties surrender instead of fighting to the death. Lords weigh odds before engaging in hopeless battles. Siege defenders can negotiate capitulation. And with *AI Executioner*, lords can execute prisoners — your enemies remember what you've done and may repay in kind.

The strategic payoff: The campaign map becomes a chessboard where moves have consequences. You cannot mindlessly attack every party you see. The AI will punish overextension. Capture and execution become strategic decisions with long-term diplomatic weight.

=== Logistics Wins Wars

*Supply Lines* adds the single most transformative strategic layer in the wave. Your army consumes supplies. Running out has consequences — morale plummets, troops desert, movement speed drops. Villages and towns become critical resupply points. You cannot march an army across the continent without planning your route.

The strategic payoff: Geography matters. A mountain pass with no villages becomes a death trap for an army low on supplies. A fertile river valley becomes a strategic asset worth fighting for. You start thinking about *where* you fight, not just *who*.

== Kingdom and Politics

=== Internal Politics

*Kingdom Politics Expanded* fixes every broken vanilla policy and makes AI voting based on actual needs rather than arbitrary weights. Lords with low-loyalty towns vote for loyalty policies. Clans contribute to wars based on their actual participation. Fief distribution tracks war scores and siege contributions.

The strategic payoff: Your kingdom runs on logic. You can predict how votes will go. You can curry favor with key clans to pass the policies you want. Politics becomes a system you can understand and manipulate, not a black box.

=== Paths to Power

*I Swear Fealty* gives vassalage ceremony and consequence. Breaking an oath matters. Swearing loyalty is a decision, not a checkbox. *BECOME KING — HOLMGANG* offers a warrior's alternative — challenge a ruler to single combat and claim the throne through personal prowess.

*Fourberie* and *Calradian's Creed* add rogue gameplay dimensions. Schemes, dirty tactics, assassination plots, stealth kills, parkour infiltration. Win through guile rather than army size. *Enlisted* offers yet another path — start as a common soldier in a lord's army and work your way up through the ranks.

The strategic payoff: Power no longer has only one door. You can marry into it, fight for it, scheme for it, or earn it through years of loyal service. Every path has different requirements, different allies, different enemies.

== Economy at Scale

=== Beyond Workshops

*Better Smithing Continued* makes smithing less grindy while keeping it balanced. Faster stamina recovery, better part unlocks, improved smelting yields. *True Battle Loot* ensures enemies drop what they were wearing — no more looting plate armor from looters. Your soldiers also claim their share, making battle rewards feel earned rather than random.

*Agriculture Estate Updated* adds farmland management — own estates, manage crop production, earn passive income. *Donate Grain* turns food surplus into diplomatic capital. Feed a starving settlement and watch your relations with local notables climb.

The strategic payoff: Your economy has multiple pillars now. If war disrupts your workshops, your estates still produce. If your caravans are raided, your smithing income covers expenses. Economic resilience translates directly into military resilience.

=== Dynastic Economics

With *Bannerlord Expanded* series mods, your family becomes an economic and political asset. Children develop with education options. Your spouse has expanded utility. Companions have depth, quests, and personality-driven behavior. Settlements have interactive depth beyond the trade/recruit/quest loop.

The strategic payoff: Your dynasty compounds. Children raised well become better lords and ladies. Spouses managed strategically provide ongoing benefits. Your clan grows in capability as it grows in size — not just more characters, but better ones.

== Battle Doctrine

=== Tactical Depth

*Reinforcement System* transforms battle pacing — distant units arrive in waves instead of spawning at your feet. Positioning matters. Holding a ridge while reinforcements stream in feels like a real engagement. *Realistic Morale System* makes morale a tactical factor — flanked units waver, leader presence inspires, casualties affect will to fight.

*Epic Sieges* adjusts siege parameters for longer, more strategic engagements. Ladders can be pushed down. Gates last longer. Siege towers are more durable. *Battle Duels* lets you challenge enemy lords to single combat during battles — resolve conflicts through personal skill.

The strategic payoff: Battles have phases now. The opening skirmish, the main engagement, the reinforcement waves, the breaking point. You fight differently when you know fresh troops are three minutes away — and so does the enemy.

=== Combat Systems

*Breakable Polearms* adds weapon durability — lances and spears break. High-quality weapons last longer. *Better Horses* reworks mounts — breed matters, horses tire, cavalry charges feel weighty. *Better Bandages* and *Better Health* make wounds meaningful — getting hurt affects combat performance, and your surgeon's skill determines recovery speed.

The strategic payoff: Equipment quality matters beyond damage numbers. A noble's lance lasts longer than a peasant's sharpened stick. Your surgeon's tent is as important as your smithy. Combat has consequences that extend beyond the battle.
